in shapes.\n  - Pair 1: the key is 4×4 at the left (rows 3–6).\n  - Pair 2: the key is 3×5 at the bottom.\n  - Test: the key is 5×4 at the left.\n\n**What changes.** Only grey cells change. Every grey shape is recoloured as a whole with a single colour. To find which colour, compare row 0 of the output lattice with row 0 of the key:\n- Pair 1: shape colours `1 8 1 8`, key row `1 8 1 8`. Shape row 1 is `8 8 1 1`, which matches key row 1.\n- Pair 2: shape colours `2 1 1 3 1`, key row `2 1 1 3 1`. Rows 1 and 2 also match.\n\nSo the key is a scaled-down picture of the answer. The shape in lattice row *r*, column *c* takes the colour of key cell (*r*, *c*). The key itself stays unchanged.\n\n**Building it generally.**\n1. Background is the most common colour.\n2. The shape colour is the most common non-background colour, which is 5 in every grid.\n3. The key is the bounding box of all cells that are neither background nor shape colour.\n4. Find the connected shape-coloured components. Their sorted distinct top-y values give the lattice row index, and their sorted distinct left-x values give the column index.\n5. Paint each shape with `key[r][c]`.\n\n**Test.** The key is 5×4, with rows `2 1 2 2 / 8 1 4 4 / 3 1 4 4 / 8 1 3 1 / 8 1 1 1`. The lattice also has 5 rows × 4 columns of shapes, so the sizes match. Each shape row is recoloured from the corresponding key row. I emit the canvas after each lattice row is painted.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    vals, counts = np.unique(g[g != bg], return_counts=True)\n    shape_col = vals[np.argmax(counts)]\n    keymask = (g != bg) & (g != shape_col)\n    ys, xs = np.nonzero(keymask)\n    key = g[ys.min():ys.max() + 1, xs.min():xs.max() + 1]\n    comps = components(g == shape_col, diagonal=True)\n    boxes = [(bbox(c), c) for c in comps]\n    rows = sorted(set(b[0] for b, _ in boxes))\n    cols = sorted(set(b[1] for b, _ in boxes))\n    out = g.copy()\n    for ri, ry in enumerate(rows):\n        for b, cells in boxes:\n            if b[0] != ry:\n                continue\n            ci = cols.index(b[1])\n            if ri < key.shape[0] and ci < key.shape[1]:\n                colour = key[ri, ci]\n                for (y, x) in cells:\n                    out[y, x] = colour\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Recolour lattice row {ri} of grey shapes using key row {ri}: {list(key[ri]) if ri < key.shape[0] else []}\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Final: every shape takes the colour of the matching key cell\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Background is the most common colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The shape colour is the most common non-background colour, which is 5 in every grid."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Every grey shape is recoloured as a whole with a single colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each shape row is recoloured from the corresponding key row."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Recolour lattice row 0 of grey shapes using key row 0: [np.int64(1), np.int64(8), np.int64(1), np.int64(8)]"}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Their sorted distinct top-y values give the lattice row index, and their sorted distinct left-x values give the column index."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The key is the bounding box of all cells that are neither background nor shape colour."}],"scale":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the key is a scaled-down picture of the answer."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"9f5efa07046fab65b3b4355c7d61f56925e80ad6ecf53e71871e8c9e74b039ce","task_sha256":"ac5e553640d49f53cc0f342565b7b42212bcf20c9013c3eca74bc20e616aaf58","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
